 ^L     | Redraw the screen
 ?      | Show help and configuration
_______ + ___________________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
On macOS, \fB;;\fR (the plugin key twice) opens the hovered file in Quick Look (\fBqlmanage\~-p\fR) and
brings its window to the front. The first use may ask for Automation permission
to control System Events.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Pp
.Nm
supports the following options:
.Pp
.Fl a
        auto-setup temporary \fBNNN_FIFO\fR (refer to the \fBENVIRONMENT\fR section)
.Pp
.Fl A
        disable directory auto-enter on unique filter match
.Pp
.Fl "b key"
        specify bookmark key to open
.Pp
.Fl B
        use bsdtar for archives (default: atool)
.Pp
.Fl c
        indicates that \fBNNN_OPENER\fR is a cli-only opener (overrides\~\fB-e\fR)
        openers invoking GUI applications should suppress their stdout/stderr
.Pp
.Fl C
        8-color scheme - color directories by context, disable file colors
.Pp
.Fl d
        detail mode
.Pp
.Fl D
        show directories in context color with \fBNNN_FCOLORS\fR set
.Pp
.Fl e
        open text files in \fBVISUAL\fR (else \fBEDITOR\fR, fallback vi) [preferably CLI]
.Pp
.Fl E
        use \fBEDITOR\fR for internal undetached edits
.Pp
.Fl f
        use history file
.Pp
.Fl "F val"
        fifo notification mode
        0: notify as previewer, 1: notify as explorer
.Pp
.Fl g
        use regex filters instead of substring match
.Pp
.Fl G
        query git and show the status column (detail and normal mode).
        Without \fB-G\fR, git is not run. Toggle with \fBi\fR.
.Pp
.Fl H
        show hidden files
.Pp
.Fl i
        show current file information in info bar (may be slow)
.Pp
.Fl I
        hide directory sizes in detail mode (files still show size;
        disk-usage sort still shows directory totals). Toggle with \fBI\fR.
.Pp
.Fl J
        disable auto-advance on selection
        (eg. selecting an entry will no longer move cursor to the next entry)
.Pp
.Fl K
        test for keybind collision and exit
.Pp
.Fl "l val"
        number of lines to move per mouse wheel scroll
.Pp
.Fl m
        hide octal permissions in detail mode (status bar still shows
        \fBls\fR-style perms). Toggle with \fBM\fR.
.Pp
.Fl n
        start in type-to-nav mode
.Pp
.Fl N
        use native prompt (instead of readline)
.Pp
.Fl o
        open files only on Enter key
.Pp
.Fl "p file"
        copy (or \fIpick\fR) selection to file, or stdout if file='-'
.Pp
.Fl "P key"
        specify plugin key to run
.Pp
.Fl Q
        disable confirmation on quit with multiple contexts active
.Pp
.Fl r
        show cp, mv progress
        (Linux-only, needs \fIadvcpmv\fR; \fB^T\fR shows the progress on BSD/macOS)
.Pp
.Fl R
        disable rollover at edges (eg. pressing \fBdown\fR while on the last
        entry will no longer move cursor to the first entry and vice\-versa)
.Pp
.Fl "s name"
        load a session by name
.Pp
.Fl S
        persistent session
.Pp
.Fl "t secs"
        idle timeout in seconds to lock terminal
.Pp
.Fl "T key"
        sort order (refer to the \fBSORTING\fR section)
        keys: 'a'pparent disk usage / 'd'isk usage / 'e'xtension / 'r'everse / 's'ize / 't'ime / 'v'ersion
        capitalize to reverse (except 'r')
.Pp
.Fl u
        use selection if available, don't prompt to choose between selection and hovered entry
.Pp
.Fl U
        show user and group names in status bar
.Pp
.Fl V
        show version and exit
.Pp
.Fl x
        show notifications on selection cp, mv, rm completion (requires the \fB.ntfy\fR plugin)
        copy path to system clipboard on selection (requires the \fB.cbcp\fR plugin)
        show xterm title (if non-picker mode)
.Pp
.Fl y
        compact timestamps in the eza default style (fixed 12 columns):
        this year as \fBDD MMM HH:MM\fR, earlier years as \fBDD MMM  YYYY\fR.
        \fBy\fR cycles ISO, eza, US and eza + today formats.
.Pp
.Fl Y
        compact timestamps in US style (fixed 12 columns):
        this year as \fBMMM DD HH:MM\fR, earlier years as \fBMMM DD  YYYY\fR
.Pp
.Fl Z
        eza style (see \fB-y\fR), but entries modified today show
        the weekday and time, e.g. \fBThu    14:32\fR, without the reverse-video highlight used for
        entries changed in the last 5 minutes (fixed 12 columns)
.Pp
.Fl z
        use fuzzy filters (fuzzy match: all characters in filter appear in order)
.Pp
.Fl 0
        use null separator (instead of newline) to separate file paths in picker mode outout
.Pp
.Fl h
        show program help and exit
.Sh CONFIGURATION
There is no configuration file. Associated files are at
.Pp
.Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/nnn/
.Pp
Configuration is done using a few optional (set if you need) environment
variables. Refer to the \fBENVIRONMENT\fR section.
.Pp
.Nm
uses \fBxdg-open\fR (on Linux), \fBopen(1)\fR (on macOS), \fBcygstart\fR on
(Cygwin) and \fBopen\fR on (Haiku) as the desktop opener. It's also possible
to specify a custom opener. Refer to the \fBENVIRONMENT\fR section.
.Sh CONTEXTS
Open multiple locations with 8 contexts. The status is shown in the top left
corner:
.Pp
- the current context is in reverse video
.br
- other active contexts are underlined
.br
- rest are inactive
.Pp
A new context copies the state of the previous context. Each context can have
its own color. Refer to the \fBENVIRONMENT\fR section.
.Sh SESSIONS
Sessions are a way to save and restore states of work. A session stores the
settings and contexts. Sessions can be loaded at runtime or with a program
option.
.Pp
- When a session is loaded at runtime, the last working state is saved
automatically to a dedicated "auto session" session file. Session option
\fIrestore\fR would restore the "auto session".
.br
- The persistent session option is global. If it is used, the last active session
will be updated with the final state at program quit.
.br
- The "auto session" is used in persistent session mode if no session is active.
.br
- Listing input stream and opening a bookmark by key have a higher priority to
session options (\fB-s\fR/\fB-S\fR).
.Pp
All the session files are located by session name in the directory
.Pp
.Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/nnn/sessions
.Pp
"@" is the "auto session" file.
.Sh FILTERS
Filters are strings (or regex patterns) to find matching entries in the current
directory instantly (\fIsearch-as-you-type\fR). Matches are case-insensitive by
default. The last filter in each context is persisted at runtime or in saved
sessions.
.br
When there's a unique match and it's a directory,
.Nm
auto enters the directory. Use the relevant program option to disable this.
.Pp
The filter prompt starts with a pill showing the mode and its keys, for example \fBstr /:\fR. Key \fB/\fR cycles
the modes at an empty prompt and \fB:\fR toggles case: lowercase (\fBstr\fR) ignores case, uppercase
(\fBSTR\fR) is case sensitive. Without pills the hint \fBstr [/], ic [:]\fR is shown on the line above.
.Bd -literal
 Pill     | Mode   | Matches                             | Option
_________ + _______ + ____________________________________ + _______
 str, STR | String | Text anywhere in the name (default) | -
 fzy, FZY | Fuzzy  | All characters in order, e.g. mvk   | -z
 reg, REG | Regex  | Regular expression                  | -g
_________ + _______ + ____________________________________ + _______
.Ed
.Pp
The regex flavour is chosen when nnn is built: POSIX extended (no lookahead) by default, PCRE2 with
\fBO_PCRE2=1\fR. The \fB?\fR help screen shows which one this build uses. Regex examples are listed below.
.Pp
Special keys at filter prompt:
.Bd -literal
 Key   | Function
______ + _______________________________________
 ^char | Usual keybind functionality
 Esc   | Exit filter prompt but skip dir refresh
 /     | Enter dir exactly matching filter
______ + _______________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
Special keys at \fBempty\fR filter prompt:
.Bd -literal
 Key  | Function
_____ + __________________________________________________________
 ?    | Show help and config screen
 /    | Toggle between string, fuzzy, regex
 :    | Toggle case-sensitivity
 ^L   | Clear filter (if prompt is \fBnon-empty\fR) OR apply last filter
 Bksp | Stay at filter prompt and refresh dir
 Del  | Stay at filter prompt and refresh dir
_____ + __________________________________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
Common regex use cases:
.Pp
(1) To list all matches starting with the filter expression,
    start the expression with a '^' (caret) symbol.
.br
(2) Type '\\.mkv' to list all MKV files.
.br
(3) Use '.*' to match any character (\fIsort of\fR fuzzy search).
.br
(4) Exclude filenames having 'nnn' (compiled with PCRE2 lib): '^(?!nnn)'
.Pp
In the \fItype-to-nav\fR mode directories are opened in filter
mode, allowing continuous navigation.
.Pp
Additional special keys at \fBempty\fR filter prompt
in \fItype-to-nav\fR mode:
.Bd -literal
 Key | Function
____ + ________________________
 '   | Go to first non-dir file
 +   | Toggle file selection
 ,   | Mark CWD
 -   | Go to last visited dir
 .   | Show hidden files
 ;   | Run a plugin by its key
 =   | Launch a GUI application
 >   | Export file list
 @   | Visit start dir
 ]   | Show command prompt
 `   | Visit /
 ~   | Go HOME
____ + ________________________
.Ed
.Sh SORTING
.Nm
will always show the directories first, followed by the files. String
order is always case-insensitive.
.Pp
The sort key can be set either with the \fB-T\fR program option, or
interactively using \fBt\fR or \fB^T\fR. The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width 2n
.It Ic a
apparent disk usage, how large the file appears to be, can be bigger than actual
disk usage in sparse files.
.It Ic d
disk usage, amount of data taken on disk, a multiple of the block size.
.It Ic e
extension of the file.
.It Ic r
reverse the current order (not available with\~\fB-T\fR).
.It Ic s
size of file, amount of data stored in the file.
.It Ic t
time, this depends on the currently selected time type which can be changed
using the \fBT\fR key.
Options are:
.Bl -tag -compact -width 1n
.It Ic a
access, last time the file was accessed.
.It Ic c
change, last time the metadata of the file was changed (e.g. permissions).
.It Ic m
modified, last time the content of the file was changed (default).
.El
.It Ic v
version, sorts by filename while treating digit characters numerically.
For example: j1.png, j02.png, j3.png.
.It Ic c
clear, revert to filename order (not available with\~\fB-T\fR).
.It Ic ^T
cycle between filename/size/time order (not available with\~\fB-T\fR).
.El
.Pp
Choosing a sort key sets it; choosing it again does not toggle it off. \fBt\fR then \fBc\fR clears
all sort keys and goes back to name order.
.Pp
The uppercase version of the option (except \fBr\fR) reverses the default order.
By default, time and size sort keys are ordered in descending order, and
alphabetical fields are ordered in ascending order.
For example, when using the \fBe\fR option, the entries will be ordered by
extension, in ascending order.
Using \fBE\fR would result in descending order of the extensions.
This means that \fBe\fR followed by \fBr\fR is the same as \fBE\fR.
.Sh SELECTION
.Nm
allows file selection across directories and contexts!
.Pp
There are 3 groups of keybinds to add files to selection:
.Pp
(1) hovered file selection toggle
    - deselects if '+' is visible before the entry, else adds to selection
.br
(2) add a range of files to selection
    - repeat the range key on the same entry twice to clear selection completely
.br
(3) add all files in the current directory to selection
.Pp
A selection can be edited, copied, moved, removed, archived or linked.
.Pp
Absolute paths of the selected files are copied to \fB.selection\fR file in
the config directory. The selection file is shared between multiple program
instances. Selection from multiple instances are not merged. The last instance
writing to the file overwrites earlier contents. If you have 2 instances of
.Nm
\fIopen\fR in 2 panes of a terminal multiplexer, you can select in one pane and
use the selection in the other pane. The selection gets cleared in the
.Nm
instance where the selection was made on mv/rm (but not on cp).
.Pp
.Nm
clears the selection after a successful operation with the selection. Plugins
are allowed to define the behaviour individually.
.Pp
To edit the selection use the _edit selection_ key. Editing doesn't end the
selection mode. You can add more files to the selection and edit the list
again. If no file is selected in the current session, this option attempts
to list the selection file.
.Pp
.Nm
can show the total size of non-filtered selected files listed in a
directory. For directories, only the size of the directory is added by
default. To add the size of the contents of a directory, sort by disk usage (aka du mode).
.Sh ARCHIVES
\fBz\fR archives the selection, or the hovered entry when nothing is selected. It asks
\fBsave archive as:\fR (prefilled with the hovered name). Type the archive's name \fBwith an extension\fR,
for example \fBphotos.zip\fR, \fBnotes.tar.gz\fR or \fBbackup.7z\fR: the extension decides the format.
A path (\fB~/backups/photos.zip\fR) says where the archive is saved; the directory should already exist. The
files are stored under their own names. If the archive exists, nnn asks before overwriting it.
.Pp
nnn does not compress anything itself; it runs the first of these tools that is installed:
.Bd -literal
 Tool   | Used when                          | Formats
_______ + ___________________________________ + _________________________
 atool  | Installed (skipped with -B)        | tar.*, zip, 7z, rar, ...
 bsdtar | No atool, or -B                    | tar.*, zip, 7z
 zip    | Neither, and the name ends in .zip | zip
 tar    | Neither of the above               | tar.*
_______ + ___________________________________ + _________________________
.Ed
.Pp
\fBatool\fR is a wrapper that calls the right program for each format. \fBbsdtar\fR is the
\fBlibarchive\fR version of tar (it is the system \fBtar\fR on macOS and many BSDs). Other tools, such
as \fB7z\fR, \fBunar\fR or \fBouch\fR, are not run directly; atool can use \fB7z\fR when it is installed.
.Pp
Opening an archive (\fBEnter\fR, \fBl\fR or \fBRight\fR) asks what to do:
.Bd -literal
 Key      | Action
_________ + ____________________________________________________________
 l, Enter | List the contents (default)
 o        | Open it like any other file (NNN_OPENER)
 x        | Extract into a directory you name (. is here), then open it
 m        | Mount it (NNN_ARCHMNT) and browse it; u unmounts
_________ + ____________________________________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
Listing and extracting use the same tools as above, in the same order (unzip for .zip when neither
atool nor bsdtar is installed). \fBNNN_ARCHIVE\fR sets which extensions count as archives.
.Pp
.Sh FIND AND LIST
There are two ways to search and list:
.Pp
- feed a list of file paths as input
.br
- search using a plugin (e.g. \fBfinder\fR) and list the results
.Pp
File paths must be NUL-separated ('\\0'). Paths and can be relative to the
current directory or absolute. Invalid paths in the input are ignored. Input
processing limit is 16,384 paths or 64 MiB (max_paths x max_path_len) of data.
.Pp
To list the input stream, start
.Nm
by writing to its standard input. E.g., to list files in current
directory larger than
1M:
.Bd -literal
    find -maxdepth 1 -size +1M -print0 | nnn
.Ed
.Pp
or redirect a list from a file:
.Bd -literal
    nnn < files.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Handy bash/zsh shell function to list files by mime-type in current directory:
.Bd -literal
    # to show video files, run: list video

    list ()
    {
        find . -maxdepth 1 | file -if- | grep "$1" | awk -F: '{printf "%s%c", $1, 0}' | nnn
    }
.Ed
.Pp
A temporary directory will be created containing symlinks to the given
paths. Any action performed on these symlinks will be performed only on their
targets, after which they might become invalid.
.Pp
\fB>\fR or \fBl\fR on a symlink in the listing dir takes to the target
file. Press \fB-\fR to return to the listing dir. Press \fBEnter\fR to open the symlink.
.Pp
Listing input stream can be scripted. It can be extended to pick (option\~-p)
selected entries from the listed results.
.Sh BOOKMARKS
There are 2 ways (can be used together) to manage bookmarks.
.Pp
(1) Bookmark keys: See \fBNNN_BMS\fR under \fBENVIRONMENT\fR section on how to set
    bookmark keys.

    The select bookmark key \fBb\fR lists all the bookmark keys set in \fBNNN_BMS\fR
    in the bookmarks prompt.
.Pp
(2) Symlinked bookmarks: A symlinked bookmark to the current directory can
    be created with the \fBB\fR key (or manually under ~/.config/nnn/bookmarks).

    Pressing \fBEnter\fR at the bookmarks prompt takes to this directory.
    If \fBNNN_BMS\fR is not set, the select bookmark key directly opens it.
.Pp
On entering a bookmark, the directory where the select bookmark key was
pressed is set as the previous directory. Press \fB-\fR to return to it.
.Pp
\fBEsc\fR at the filter prompt that opens in the bookmarks directory goes back to
the directory you came from.
.Pp
.Sh APPEARANCE
With a nerd font build (\fBO_NERD\fR) the interface is drawn with rounded pills
and a few small visual touches that make the listing easier to scan:
.Pp
- the hovered entry, the status line segments (counter, selection, sort and
filter flags, time, permissions, owner, size, file name), the context number
and path in the top line, and every message and prompt are pills with rounded
caps, \fBNNN_PILL\fR, \fBNNN_MSGCOLOR\fR
.br
- the status line segments and their order are set with \fBNNN_STATUS\fR; on a narrow
screen the ones that do not fit are dropped from the right
.br
- the git branch and its summary follow the path in the top line, \fBNNN_GITBRANCH\fR
.br
- a triangle marks the hovered entry in the detail view, \fBNNN_HOVERMARK\fR
.br
- recently modified entries get a clock icon before the time, \fBNNN_YOUNG\fR
.br
- timestamps can be compact, \fB-y\fR, \fB-Y\fR, \fB-Z\fR, and key
.Ic y
toggles the options
.br
- the time and size columns have their own colors, \fBNNN_FCOLORS\fR
.br
- on a narrow screen the detail view gives way to the light view and returns when the
window is wide enough again (\fBd\fR keeps it off)
.br
- choice prompts and floating windows are described in the next two sections
.Pp
Without a nerd font build there are no pills, the hovered entry is marked with
\fB>\fR and recently modified timestamps are shown in reverse video.
.Sh CHOICE PROMPTS
Prompts with options (new file/dir/symlink/hardlink, copy/move as, current/selection,
GUI/CLI, session, remote and archive open) show one pill per option. The default
option, the one \fBEnter\fR picks, is the filled message pill and the others use the context color.
The key to press is capitalised and underlined (\f(BIF\fBile\fR, \f(BID\fBir\fR, \fBe\f(BIX\fBtract\fR).
A final \fBEsc\fR pill shows that any other key cancels.
.Pp
\fBLeft\fR and \fBRight\fR move the filled pill along the options and the \fBEsc\fR pill (wrapping around);
\fBEnter\fR picks the filled one, and typing an option's key still works.
.Pp
Needs the rounded pills; on a narrow screen the options that do not fit are left off.
Without pills (\fBNNN_PILL=0\fR) the plain \fB'f'ile/'d'ir\fR text is shown.
.Sh FLOATING WINDOWS
Help, file stats (\fBf\fR) and confirmations open in rounded, titled popups that
fit their content. Scroll indicators are nerd font carets (\fB^v<>\fR without nerd font
support) and the key hints along the bottom are pills with a bold key.
.Pp
On macOS \fBf\fR uses GNU \fBstat\fR from Homebrew coreutils
(\fB/opt/homebrew/opt/coreutils/libexec/gnubin/stat\fR) when it is installed, otherwise \fB/usr/bin/stat -x\fR.
.Pp
\fBp\fR (copy) and \fBv\fR (move) with nothing selected show a popup first:
\fBEnter\fR opens the editor on a blank path list, \fBq\fR or \fBEsc\fR cancels
without opening it.
.Pp
\fBx\fR and \fBX\fR on a single file end the prompt with \fB[y/n]\fR. With a selection,
the files are listed in a popup titled with the count: \fBy\fR deletes (or trashes with
\fBNNN_TRASH\fR), \fBn\fR, \fBq\fR or \fBEsc\fR cancels.
.Sh GIT STATUS
Git status is built into
.Nm
and needs no patch. \fB-G\fR queries git and adds a status column to the detail and
normal views, and \fBi\fR toggles it in a running instance. Without \fB-G\fR git is not run.
The column appears only when git reports a change. It has two cells, for staged and
unstaged changes. Ignored entries are drawn dim and italic and get no mark, and a folder
is dimmed only when the whole folder is ignored. \fBNNN_GITIGNORE=mark\fR marks them
\fB!!\fR instead, and a folder then shows the first status found under it, ignored files included.
.Pp
.Bd -literal
 Nerd font | Plain | Meaning
__________ + _____ + _________
          | A     | Added
          | D     | Deleted
          | M     | Modified
 ?         | ?     | Untracked
          | U     | Unmerged
          | !     | Ignored
__________ + _____ + _________
.Ed
.Pp
With a nerd font build and pills, the current branch and a summary follow the path in the top
line (\fB+\fRstaged \fB~\fRunstaged \fB!\fRconflicts \fB?\fRuntracked \fB\(ua\fRahead
\fB\(da\fRbehind). It is green when the repository is clean and red when there are changes.
It is shown whenever the status column is on, so \fB-G\fR and \fBi\fR turn it on and off too.
\fBNNN_GITBRANCH=0\fR hides it.
.Pp
Inside \fB$HOME\fR it works with a bare dotfiles repository.
.Sh CHANGES
This is the amazebb fork of nnn. Everything upstream does still works; the fork adds
the following (the last column is the section of this manual that explains it):
.Bd -literal
 Area           | Change                          | Section
_______________ + ________________________________ + _________________
 Pills          | Rounded pills, hover to prompts | APPEARANCE
 Status line    | Pills, order set by NNN_STATUS  | APPEARANCE
 Filter prompt  | One pill: mode and case         | FILTERS
 Choice prompts | Pills, Left/Right to select     | CHOICE PROMPTS
 Popups         | Rounded help, stats, confirms   | FLOATING WINDOWS
 Git            | Built-in column, branch segment | GIT STATUS
 Timestamps     | Compact styles, young entries   | APPEARANCE
 Detail view    | Keys i M y I; returns on widen  | KEYBINDS
 Sorting        | t sets a key, T S E reverse     | SORTING
 Open with      | o: GUI/CLI choice, not found    | KEYBINDS
 Archives       | z, and l o x m on Enter         | ARCHIVES
 Bookmarks      | Esc goes back                   | BOOKMARKS
 Clipboard      | C, ^B copy; ;; Quick Look       | KEYBINDS
 Help (?)       | Archive, filter keys, regex     | KEYBINDS
_______________ + ________________________________ + _________________
.Ed
.Pp
Plugins: \fBbb-z\fR (\fB;z\fR) jumps to a zoxide match (an empty prompt opens \fBzoxide query -i\fR,
otherwise the words you type are the query), and \fBpreview-tui\fR has native splits in
Ghostty and Rex, image previews with chafa or viu and an EXIF caption, and camera RAW previews.
Both are in the \fBplugins\fR folder of the repository.
.Pp
.Sh UNITS
The minimum file size unit is byte (B). The rest are K, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y
(powers of 1024), same as the default units in \fBls\fR.
.Pp
The units are listed below.
.Pp
.Bd -literal
 Unit | Name      | Size
_____ + _________ + ______
 B    | Byte      | 1
 K    | Kilobyte  | 1024
 M    | Megabyte  | 1024^2
 G    | Gigabyte  | 1024^3
 T    | Terabyte  | 1024^4
 P    | Petabyte  | 1024^5
 E    | Exabyte   | 1024^6
 Z    | Zettabyte | 1024^7
 Y    | Yottabyte | 1024^8
_____ + _________ + ______
.Ed
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The \fBSHELL\fR, \fBVISUAL\fR (else \fBEDITOR\fR) and \fBPAGER\fR environment
variables are used. A single combination of arguments is supported for
\fBSHELL\fR and \fBPAGER\fR.
.Pp
\fBNNN_OPTS:\fR options to
.Nm
, parsed like the command line. The leading '-' is optional. Options passed
on the command line override NNN_OPTS.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_OPTS="cEnrx"
    export NNN_OPTS="cEnrx -T t"
    export NNN_OPTS="-HdT t"
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_OPENER:\fR specify a custom file opener.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_OPENER=nuke
.Ed
.Pp
NOTE: \fBnuke\fR is a file opener available in the plugin repository.
.Pp
\fBNNN_BMS:\fR bookmark string as \fIkey_char:location\fR pairs
separated by \fB;\fR:
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_BMS="d:$HOME/Docs;u:/home/user/Cam Uploads;D:$HOME/Downloads/"
.Ed
.Pp
    These bookmarks are listed in the help and config screen (key \fB?\fR).
.Pp
\fBNNN_PLUG:\fR directly executable plugins as \fIkey_char:plugin\fR pairs
separated by \fB;\fR:
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='f:finder;o:fzopen;p:mocplay;d:diffs;t:nmount;v:imgview'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
\fBNOTES\fR
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
To run a plugin directly, press \fB;\fR followed by the key.
.It
Alternatively, combine with \fBAlt\fR (i.e. \fBAlt+key\fR).
.It
To pick and run an unassigned plugin, press \fBEnter\fR at the plugin prompt.
.It
To run a plugin at startup, use the option\~\fB-P\fR followed by the plugin key.
.El
.Ed
.Pp
\fBPLUGIN MODIFIERS\fR
.Pp
.Bq Ic -
To skip directory refresh after running a plugin, prefix\~with\~\fB-\fR.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='p:-plugin'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bq Ic \&!
To assign keys to arbitrary non-background cli commands and invoke like
plugins, add \fB!\fR before the command.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='x:!chmod +x "$nnn";g:-!git log'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bq Ic &
To run a \fIGUI app as plugin\fR, add a \fB&\fR after \fB!\fR.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='m:-!&mousepad "$nnn"'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bq Ic \&|
To show the output of run-and-exit commands which do not need user input,
add \fB|\fR (pipe) after \fB!\fR.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='m:-!|mediainfo "$nnn";t:-!|tree -ps;l:-!|ls -lah --group-directories-first'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
incompatible with \fB&\fR (terminal output is masked for GUI programs)
.El
.Pp
.Bq Ic >
To show the output of run-and-exit commands in a floating window,
add \fB>\fR (right arrow) after \fB!\fR.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='m:!>mediainfo "$nnn";t:!>tree -ps;l:!>ls -lah --group-directories-first'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
incompatible with \fB&\fR (terminal output is masked for GUI programs)
.It
ignores \fB-\fR (the directory is always refreshed)
.El
.Pp
.Bq Ic *
To skip user confirmation after command execution, suffix with \fB*\fR.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PLUG='y:-!sync*'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
should be placed at the end of the plugin/command string
.It
ignored if \fB|\fR is used (output is already paged for user)
.It
ignored if \fB>\fR is used (output is rendered in a floating window)
.It
do not use \fB*\fR with programs those run and exit e.g. 'cat'
.El
.Pp
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
\fBNOTES\fR
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Place $nnn (or exported variables) in double quotes (\fB"$nnn"\fR).
.It
Use single quotes for $NNN_PLUG so "$nnn" is not interpreted.
.El
.Ed
.Pp
\fBEXAMPLES\fR
.Pp
.Bd -literal
 Key:Command                         | Description
____________________________________ + ________________________________
 c:!convert "$nnn" png:- |           | Copy image to clipboard
 xclip -sel clipboard -t image/png*  |
 C:!cp -rv "$nnn" "$nnn".cp*         | Create a copy of the hovered
                                     | file
 e:-!sudo -E vim "$nnn"*             | Edit file as root in vim
 g:-!git diff                        | Show git diff
 h:-!hx "$nnn"*                      | Open hovered file in hx hex
                                     | editor
 k:-!fuser -kiv "$nnn"*              | Interactively kill process(es)
                                     | using hovered file
 l:-!git log                         | Show git log
 n:-!vi /home/user/Dropbox/dir/note* | Take quick notes in a synced
                                     | file/dir of notes
 p:-!less -iR "$nnn"*                | Page through hovered file in
                                     | less
 s:-!&smplayer -minigui "$nnn"       | Play hovered media file, even
                                     | unfinished download
 x:!chmod +x "$nnn"                  | Make the hovered file executable
 y:-!sync*                           | Flush cached writes
____________________________________ + ________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
Online docs: https://github.com/jarun/nnn/tree/master/plugins
.Pp
\fBNNN_ORDER:\fR directory-specific sort key.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_ORDER='t:/home/user/Downloads;S:/tmp'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
\fBNOTES\fR
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Sort keys can be a/d/e/r/s/t/v (refer to the \fBSORTING\fR section).
.It
Path must be absolute.
.El
.Ed
.Pp
Timestamps of entries modified/created within 5 minutes are shown in reverse.
.Pp
\fBNNN_COLORS:\fR string of color numbers for each context, e.g.:
.Pp
8 color numbers (0-black, 1-red, 2-green, 3-yellow, 4-blue (default), 5-magenta, 6-cyan, 7-white).
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_COLORS='12341234'
.Ed
.Pp
xterm 256 color numbers (converted to hex, 2 symbols per context). See
https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1482942/93023823-46a6ba80-f5e1-11ea-9ea3-6a3c757704f4.png
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_COLORS='#0a1b2c3d0a1b2c3d'
.Ed
.Pp
Both (256 followed by 8 as fallback, separated by ';').
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_COLORS='#0a1b2c3d0a1b2c3d;12341234'
.Ed
.Pp
NOTE: If only 256 colors are specified and the terminal doesn't support, default is used.
.Pp
\fBNNN_FCOLORS:\fR specify file-type specific colors:
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_FCOLORS='c1e2272e006033f7c6d6abc404b3'
.Ed
.Pp
Specify file-specific colors in xterm 256 color hex numbers (2 symbols per color).
Order is strict, use 00 to omit/use default terminal color. Defaults:
.Pp
.Bd -literal
 Order                     | Hex | Color
__________________________ + ___ + _______________
 Block device              | c1  | DarkSeaGreen1
 Char device               | e2  | Yellow1
 Directory                 | 27  | DeepSkyBlue1
 Executable                | 2e  | Green1
 Regular                   | 00  | Normal
 Hard link                 | 60  | Plum4
 Symbolic link             | 33  | Cyan1
 Missing OR file details   | f7  | Grey62
 Orphaned symbolic link    | c6  | DeepPink1
 FIFO                      | d6  | Orange1
 Socket                    | ab  | MediumOrchid1
 Unknown OR 0B regular/exe | c4  | Red1
 Detail time               | 04  | Blue
 Detail size               | b3  | LightGoldenrod3
__________________________ + ___ + _______________
.Ed
.Pp
The last two are optional; if omitted or 00, they use the file details color.
.Pp
If the terminal supports xterm 256 colors or more, file-specific colors will be rendered.
To force the 8-color scheme use option\~\fB-C\fR.
If xterm 256 colors aren't supported, 8-color scheme will be used.
.Pp
\fBNNN_ARCHIVE:\fR archive extensions to be handled silently (default: bzip2, (g)zip, tar).
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_ARCHIVE="\\\\.(7z|bz2|gz|tar|tgz|zip)$"
.Ed
.Pp
NOTE: Non-default formats may require a third-party utility.
.Pp
\fBNNN_ARCHMNT:\fR optional archive mounter utility (default: archivemount).
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_ARCHIVE='fuse-archive'
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_SSHFS:\fR specify custom sshfs command with options:
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_SSHFS='sshfs -o reconnect,idmap=user,cache_timeout=3600'
.Ed
.Pp
NOTE: The options must be comma-separated without any space between them.
.Pp
\fBNNN_RCLONE:\fR pass additional options to rclone command:
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_RCLONE='rclone mount --read-only --no-checksum'
.Ed
.Pp
NOTE: The options must be preceded by "rclone" and max 5 flags are supported.
.Pp
\fBNNN_TRASH:\fR trash (instead of \fBrm -rf\fR) files to desktop Trash.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_TRASH=cmd
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
\fBNOTES\fR
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
\fBcmd\fR is the name/path of the binary that nnn will call for
trashing files. E.g to use macOS's native `trash' command, set
\fBNNN_TRASH="trash"\fR.
.It
Special value "1" and "2" for cmd will use 'trash-cli' and 'gio trash' respectively.
.El
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_SEL:\fR absolute path to custom selection file.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_SEL='/tmp/.sel'
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_FIFO:\fR path of a named pipe to write the hovered file path:
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_FIFO='/tmp/nnn.fifo'
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
\fBNOTES\fR
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
Overridden by a temporary path with the\~\fB-a\fR option.
.It
If the FIFO file doesn't exist it will be created,
but not removed (unless it is generated by the\~\fB-a\fR option).
.El
.Ed
.Pp
Online docs: https://github.com/jarun/nnn/wiki/Live-previews
.Pp
\fBNNN_LOCKER:\fR terminal locker program.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_LOCKER='bmon -p wlp1s0'
    export NNN_LOCKER='cmatrix'
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_TMPFILE:\fR \fIalways\fR cd on quit and write the command in the file specified.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_TMPFILE='/tmp/.lastd'
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_HELP:\fR run a program and show the output on top of the program help page.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_HELP='fortune'
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_MCLICK:\fR key emulated by a middle mouse click.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_MCLICK='^R'
.Ed
.Pp
NOTE: Only the first character is considered if not a \fBCtrl+key\fR combo.
.Pp
\fBNNN_PILL:\fR set to \fB0\fR to turn off the rounded caps (pills) on the hovered entry,
status line, top line and prompts (on by default; needs a nerd font build, \fBO_NERD\fR).
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_PILL=0
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_STATUS:\fR segments of the status line after the file counter and selection, as letters from left to right.
A segment left out is hidden. With pills, the segment that no longer fits and all after it are dropped on a
narrow screen, so the order is the priority. Default \fBftspon\fR.
.Bd -literal
 Letter | Segment
_______ + ______________________
 f      | Sort and filter flags
 t      | Time
 s      | Size
 p      | Permissions
 o      | Owner (needs -U)
 n      | File name
_______ + ______________________
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_STATUS=tsn
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_MSGCOLOR:\fR xterm color number (0-255) of the message and prompt pills
(default: 208, orange). Needs a 256-color terminal.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_MSGCOLOR=141
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_YOUNG:\fR highlight style of timestamps modified in the last 5 minutes.
.Bd -literal
 Value   | Meaning
________ + ______________________________________________________________
 reverse | Reverse video (default without O_NERD)
 pill    | Reverse video with rounded caps in the neighbouring cells
 icon    | Clock icon before the time, no highlight (default with O_NERD)
 none    | No highlight
________ + ______________________________________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_YOUNG=pill
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_HOVERMARK:\fR marker before the time column of the hovered entry in detail mode.
.Bd -literal
 Value    | Meaning
_________ + __________________________________
 triangle | Filled caret (default with O_NERD)
 1        | '>' (default without O_NERD)
 0        | No marker
_________ + __________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_HOVERMARK=0
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_GITBRANCH:\fR set to \fB0\fR to hide the git segment after the path in the top line
(on by default; needs the rounded pills and a 256-color terminal). The segment shows the branch and
the same summary as the zsh prompt (\fB+\fRstaged \fB~\fRunstaged \fB!\fRconflicts \fB?\fRuntracked
\fB\(ua\fRahead \fB\(da\fRbehind), green when clean and red when there are changes. It is read from
the \fBdotfiles\fR zsh function in \fB~/.local/share/zsh/site-functions/dotfiles/dotfiles\fR (which also
selects the bare \fB~/.dotfiles\fR repository in tracked folders); without that file nothing is shown.
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_GITBRANCH=0
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNNN_GITIGNORE:\fR how git ignored entries are shown (needs \fB-G\fR). Only an entry that is ignored itself
is styled, so a folder that merely contains ignored files is not, unless the whole folder is ignored.
.Bd -literal
 Value   | Meaning
________ + __________________________________________________
 (unset) | Dim and italic, in their own colors (default)
 mark    | Marked !! in the git column, rolled up to folders
________ + __________________________________________________
.Ed
.Pp
.Bd -literal
    export NNN_GITIGNORE=mark
.Ed
.Pp
\fBNO_COLOR:\fR disable ANSI color output (overridden by \fBNNN_COLORS\fR).
.Pp
\fBnnn:\fR a special variable that you cannot set. It is set to the name of the hovered
file before the command prompt starts, a shell is spawned or a plugin runs, so
commands can refer to it as \fB"$nnn"\fR.
